			<div role="main" class="cf">
					<div class="post-12290 page type-page status-publish has-post-thumbnail hentry grow-content-body">
				<span id="dpsp-post-sticky-bar-markup" data-mobile-size="720"></span><span id="dpsp-post-content-markup" data-image-pin-it="false"></span><div class="row wpb_row row-fluid"><div class="wpb_column columns medium-9 small-12"><div class="vc_column-inner  "><div class="wpb_wrapper"><aside class="gap cf" style="height:50px;"></aside>
	<div class="wpb_text_column wpb_content_element">
		<div class="wpb_wrapper">
			<h1>P.R.I.C.E. Treatment</h1>
<p><a href="https://www.sportsmd.com/about-us/medical-authors-contributors/terry-zeigler-edd-atc/" data-wpel-link="internal">By Terry Zeigler, EdD, ATC</a></p>
Last Updated on August 12, 2024 by <a href="" target="_blank" class="last-modified-author" data-wpel-link="internal">The SportsMD Editors</a>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Hundreds of athletes sustain acute injuries everyday which can be treated safely at home using the P.R.I.C.E treatment. An acute sports injury is one in which there is a specific mechanism of injury (hit by ball, plant and twist, collision with opponent) and an immediate onset of symptoms including swelling, pain, bleeding, and possible deformity.</p>
<p>A number of types of sports injuries can be considered acute including:<br>
&bull; Sprains (injury to ligaments)<br>
&bull; Strains (injury to muscles)<br>
&bull; Contusions (bruise)<br>
&bull; Subluxation (partial dislocation that reduces itself)<br>
&bull; Dislocation<br>
&bull; Fractures</p>
<p>If there are signs or symptoms of a serious injury (deformity, severe swelling and/or pain), emergency first aid should be provided while keeping the athlete calm and still until emergency service personnel arrive on the scene.</p>
<figure id="attachment_13908" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-13908" style="width: 535px" class="wp-caption alignright"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class=" wp-image-13908" src="https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate.jpg" alt="PRICE protection rest ice compression elevate" width="535" height="356" srcset="https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate.jpg 1920w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-300x200.jpg 300w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-768x512.jpg 768w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-200x133.jpg 200w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-400x267.jpg 400w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-600x400.jpg 600w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-800x533.jpg 800w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/01/PRICE-protection-rest-ice-compression-elevate-1200x800.jpg 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 535px) 100vw, 535px"/><figcaption id="caption-attachment-13908" class="wp-caption-text">Image Source: www.bupa.co.uk</figcaption></figure>
<p>However, most acute sports injuries can be treated safely at home using the P.R.I.C.E. principle. The acronym stands for:</p>
<p><span class="ADetailtext">&bull; Protection</span></p>
<p><span class="ADetailtext">&bull; Rest<br>
&bull; Ice<br>
&bull; Compression<br>
&bull; Elevation<br>
</span></p>
<p>The principles of P.R.I.C.E. should be used for the first 48 &ndash; 72 hours immediately after the injury. The goal during this time frame is to control the amount of swelling to the injured area, prevent further injury, and reduce pain. Following these principles can effectively reduce the amount of swelling in an injured area thereby reducing the amount of time required for rehabilitation.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2><span class="ADetailtext"><b>Protection</b></span></h2>
<p>The first principle is protection. The purpose of protection is to avoid further injury to the area by protecting the injured structures. The type of protection used varies depending on the injured area but may include an <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/3aLagYP" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">ace bandage</a></strong>, <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/2WOQZOi" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">aluminum splint</a></strong>, <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/3rvauJK" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">sling</a></strong>, <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/34LNT1N" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">protective tape</a></strong>, or over-the-counter brace.</p>
<p>These devices are applied so that the injured structures are protected from further injury. For example, an individual who recently suffered an <a href="https://www.sportsmd.com/sports-injuries/foot-ankle-injuries/sprained-ankle/" data-wpel-link="internal">ankle sprain</a> may initially wear an <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/3aIL19D" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">ankle brace</a></strong>. A good ankle brace should protect the athlete from movements of inversion (movement of the ankle/foot inwards) and eversion (movement of the ankle/foot outwards) because those movements could further injure the damaged ligaments, but would allow the ankle/foot to move up and down.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2><span class="ADetailtext"><b>Rest</b></span></h2>
<p>Rest is the second component of the P.R.I.C.E. principle. The purpose of resting is to allow the body&rsquo;s own healing processes to naturally occur without being impeded by movement of the injured area. Any increase in movement of an injured tissue results in increased circulation to the area which in turn may result in further damage to the injured tissue and/or increased swelling.</p>
<p>For injuries to the lower extremities, crutches may be a good option for resting the injured area. If an athlete is in enough pain to have a noticeable limp, the athlete should be placed on <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/3o4mAaN" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">crutches</a></strong>. The crutches provide a safe means for the athlete to move around while ensuring that the damaged area is not stressed.</p>
<p>Athletes can either be non-weight bearing on crutches (injured leg is bent with full weight of athlete on crutches and uninjured leg while walking) or partial-weight bearing (partial weight is placed on injured leg). Athletes may use a combination of these two gaits starting with non-weight bearing for the first 48hours and then moving to a partial-weight bearing gait.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2><span class="ADetailtext"><b>Ice</b></span></h2>
<p>Ice is another component of the P.R.I.C.E. principle. There are a number of types of cryotherapy that can be used effectively to treat injuries.</p>
<p>Ideally, <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/3pkITJa" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">ice packs</a></strong> are made of crushed ice because the crushed ice is more comfortable for the athletes and conforms to the contours of the injured area better than cubed ice. Ice can be placed into plastic or Ziploc bags. A light barrier should be placed between the skin and the ice bag (paper towel) to prevent injury to the skin during the application of the ice.</p>
<p>The ice pack can be secured with an <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/37Q2aN1" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">ace bandage</a></strong> if needed. The ice should be applied for 20 minutes at a time and then removed. This can be repeated every two hours while the athlete is awake.</p>
<p>Some athletes are hypersensitive to ice or may actually be allergic to ice. In this case, the athlete&rsquo;s skin may become red, raised, and blotchy where the ice made contact with the skin. If this occurs, immediately remove the ice pack and let the area rewarm.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2><span class="ADetailtext"><b>Compression</b></span></h2>
<p>When the ice pack is removed, a <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/38E8RAK" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">compression wrap</a></strong> should be applied to the injured area. The compression wrap serves as a mechanical barrier so that swelling is minimized in the injured area. There are a number of compression wraps available on the market, but the most commonly used is an <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/37QcPqY" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">elastic</a></strong> or ace bandage.</p>
<p>Elastic bandages come in a number of sizes (2&rdquo;, 3&rdquo;, 4&rdquo;, and 6&rdquo;) and should be selected depending upon the body part that needs to be wrapped. A two inch elastic bandage is ideal for an adult&rsquo;s wrist/hand or a child&rsquo;s ankle.</p>
<p>Three and four inch elastic bandages can be used for an adult&rsquo;s ankle or elbow. Six inch elastic bandages are perfect for the application of compression to the thigh for a quadriceps or hamstring injury.</p>
<p>To wrap the knee, groin, or shoulder area, the preferred elastic bandage is a double length elastic bandage. This double-length wrap can be purchased in four and six inch widths.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2><span class="ADetailtext"><b>Elevation</b></span></h2>
<p>The last component of the P.R.I.C.E. principle is elevation. Elevation is important immediately post-injury to reduce the amount of blood flow to the injured area. For the lower extremities, the athlete can elevate his/her leg by lying down and elevating the injured limb on <strong><a href="https://amzn.to/2KUpbp0" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">pillows</a></strong>. The key is that the athlete needs to have the injured area above his/her heart level.</p>
<p>Following the PRICE principles is an effective way to minimize the swelling in an injured area so that the athlete can return to play quickly.</p>
<div class="vc_separator wpb_content_element vc_separator_align_center vc_sep_border_width_1 vc_sep_pos_align_left vc_separator_no_text wpb_content_element  wpb_content_element" style="width: 100%;"><span class="vc_sep_holder vc_sep_holder_l"><span class="vc_sep_line"></span></span><span class="vc_sep_holder vc_sep_holder_r"><span class="vc_sep_line"></span></span>
</div><aside class="gap cf" style="height:25px;"></aside><br>
<div class="vc_btn3-container vc_btn3-left vc_do_btn"><a onmouseenter="this.style.backgroundColor=&#039;#0074ad&#039;; this.style.color=&#039;#f7f7f7&#039;;" onmouseleave="this.style.backgroundColor=&#039;#0088cc&#039;; this.style.color=&#039;#fff&#039;;" style="background-color:#0088cc; color:#fff;" class="vc_general vc_btn3 vc_btn3-size-md vc_btn3-shape-round vc_btn3-style-classic" href="https://virtualcare.sportsmd.com" title="" target="_blank" data-wpel-link="external" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">Telehealth appointments within 24 hours</a></div>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2>When to See a <a class="glossaryLink" aria-describedby="tt" data-cmtooltip="&lt;div class=glossaryItemTitle&gt;Sports Medicine Doctor&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=glossaryItemBody&gt;&lt;img width=&#039;100&#039; height=&#039;59&#039; src=&#039;https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/p-img01.jpg&#039; class=&#039;cmtt-tooltip-featured-image wp-post-image&#039; alt=&#039;What is a Sports Medicine Doctor&#039; style=&#039;width: 100px; margin-top: 2%;float: right; margin-left: 2%;&#039; sizes=&#039;(max-width: 9999px) 100px,auto&#039; srcset=&#039;https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/p-img01.jpg 485w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/p-img01-300x177.jpg 300w&#039; /&gt; A sports medicine doctor is a primary care doctor who is specially trained to diagnose and treat injuries that are sustained while playing sports and any other type of&nbsp; musculoskeletal(...)&amp;lt;/strong&amp;gt;&lt;/div&gt;" href="https://www.sportsmd.com/glossary/sports-medicine-doctor/" data-mobile-support="0" data-gt-translate-attributes="[{&quot;attribute&quot;:&quot;data-cmtooltip&quot;, &quot;format&quot;:&quot;html&quot;}]" tabindex="0" role="link" data-wpel-link="internal">Sports Medicine Doctor</a></h2>
<p>Everyday hundreds of athletes sustain suden trauma injuries &ndash; or an injury due to overuse. There are definately signs when you should seek an appointment with a sports medicine doctor to diagnose the injury and establish a treatment plan.&nbsp; These signs can include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Swelling.</strong> Many sport injuries result in significant swelling of a joint, such as a ankle or knee. If the swelling is accompanied by bruising, tenderness, or a deformed limb, it could be a sign of a broken bone and time to see a doctor.</li>
<li><strong>Inability to Bear Weight or put pressure on the Joint.</strong> If you&rsquo;re unable to bear weight or put pressure on a joint, you may have a serious orthopedic injury.</li>
<li><strong>Lingering Pain After a Period of Rest.</strong> Rest your body and injury for a week and if the pain still continues it&rsquo;s time to see a sports medicine doctor.</li>
<li><strong>Inability to Move or Bend a Joint.</strong>&nbsp; If you are unable to bend or move a joint, such as an ankle, elbow, wrist, shoulder, back&nbsp; or knee, the cause is likely an orthopedic injury and you should seek care from a dooctor.</li>
<li>
</ul>
<h2>Get a Virtual Sports Specialized appointment within 5 minutes for $29</h2>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-19744 size-medium perfmatters-lazy" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='300'%20height='300'%20viewBox='0%200%20300%20300'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Orthopedic Virtual Care" width="300" height="300" data-src="https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-300x300.png" data-srcset="https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-300x300.png 300w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-150x150.png 150w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-768x768.png 768w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-180x180.png 180w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-90x90.png 90w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-450x450.png 450w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-270x270.png 270w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-20x20.png 20w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-225x225.png 225w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor.png 900w, https://www.sportsmd.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/Doctor-96x96.png 96w" data-sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" />When you have questions like: I have an injury and how should I manage it?&nbsp; How severe is it and should I get medical care from urgent care center or hospital?&nbsp; Who can I talk to right now?&nbsp; SportsMD Virtual Urgent Care is available by phone or video anytime, anywhere 24/7/365 and appointments within 5 minutes.. <strong>Learn more via <a href="https://virtualcare.sportsmd.com/" data-wpel-link="external" target="_blank" rel="nofollow external noopener noreferrer">SportsMD&rsquo;s Virtual Urgent Care Service</a>.</strong></p>
